{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I understand you’ve been having abdominal pain since your test today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it started during the MRCP when they gave me that secretin shot. It was sharp, right here in the upper belly, but only lasted a few minutes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And then it came back later?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "About two hours after I got home. It got worse—radiated to my back, with nausea. I took my usual Dilaudid, but it didn’t help much."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That sounds like a flare of pancreatitis, especially given your history. We’ll keep you NPO for now—nothing by mouth—to let your pancreas rest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Again? But the pain was only brief during the test. Is this really pancreatitis?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, secretin can trigger it, even if the test itself is safe for most. Your symptoms are classic—epigastric pain, nausea, radiation to the back. We’ve started IV fluids and will give you IV pain meds to help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. My lipase was only 40 in the ER—is that high enough?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2149-10-31 21:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s on the lower side, but we don’t always see big enzyme rises, especially with mild or early episodes. Your history and symptoms guide us more here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2149-10-31 23:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just checking in. How’s the pain now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2149-10-31 23:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Much better. The IV meds helped. I’m not nauseous anymore."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2149-10-31 23:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. We’ll start you on clear liquids tomorrow if you stay comfortable. We also reviewed your MRCP—no changes in the cysts, and your pancreatic duct responded normally to secretin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2149-10-31 23:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Are the cysts still there?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2149-10-31 23:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, but they’re small and unchanged—8 mm and under, no signs of concern. The biliary irregularities we saw before are also less noticeable, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2149-11-01 09:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well overnight. You’re pain-free and tolerating sips. We’ll advance you to a soft diet and get ready for discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2149-11-01 09:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. What about my other meds? I take Creon, omeprazole, and the ones for my blood pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2149-11-01 09:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll continue all of them. We’re adjusting your fludrocortisone to half dose—0.1 mg twice daily—because you tend to get low potassium. Midodrine you’ll restart at home when you’re up and moving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2149-11-01 09:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my antidepressant and amitriptyline?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2149-11-01 09:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, both continue. We lowered amitriptyline to 25 mg at night to help with side effects, but it’s still important for sleep and nerve-related pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2149-11-01 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs are stable, and you’ve eaten without pain. We’re clearing you to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2149-11-01 10:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What should I watch for?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2149-11-01 10:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Avoid fatty and high-protein foods for a few days. Reintroduce them slowly. If you develop fever, chills, or vomiting, come back right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2149-11-01 10:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2149-11-01 10:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll schedule a visit with your gastroenterologist to review the MRCP and your pancreatitis history. Keep taking Creon with all meals—six capsules now, as prescribed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2149-11-01 10:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’m glad it wasn’t worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2149-11-01 10:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You responded well. Just take it easy the next few days, stay hydrated, and follow the diet plan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2149-11-01 13:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your discharge papers are ready. You’re cleared to leave whenever you’re ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2149-11-01 13:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Appreciate all the help. I’ll follow up as scheduled."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2149-10-31 20:45:00",
    "admission_end": "2149-11-01 14:15:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with acute pancreatitis triggered by secretin administration during an MRCP, presenting with epigastric pain radiating to the back and nausea. She was managed with NPO status, IV fluids, and IV pain control, then gradually advanced to a soft diet as symptoms resolved. Her pancreatic cysts remained stable on imaging, and biliary irregularities improved. Fludrocortisone was adjusted to 0.1 mg BID to prevent hypokalemia, and midodrine was held during admission per patient preference. She was discharged home in good condition, tolerating oral intake, with instructions to avoid fatty foods and monitor for fever or vomiting.",
    "problems": [
      "Acute pancreatitis",
      "Von Willebrand's disease",
      "Unspecified intestinal malabsorption",
      "Sicca syndrome",
      "Iron deficiency anemia, unspecified",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified"
    ]
  }
}
